"""
Generate the scraper table of README.md (and of its translations in docs/README.<language>.md), llms.txt and the
README.md of every scraper folder from the scraper folders, so none of them is edited by hand.

    python .github/scripts/docs.py                        # rewrite the README tables and llms.txt
    python .github/scripts/docs.py --check                # exit 1 when one of them is out of date
    python .github/scripts/docs.py --status status.json   # also order the README rows by the last nightly run

Where each part comes from:
- one README row and one llms.txt entry for every <name>-scraper folder; llms.txt is sorted by website name, the
  README rows keep the order they have (new folders go last) unless --status is given the status.json that
  .github/scripts/ci_status.py writes to the ci-status branch: then they are ordered by the last nightly run,
  passing scrapers first, then partly passing, failing and not run ones (the nightly's status job does this);
- .github/scrapers.json (one entry per folder): website name, `summary` ("rental listing data from Airbnb.com"),
  `scrapes` (the "What it scrapes" list, markdown lines), `notes` (extra markdown paragraphs), scrape guides (link and
  title), sample dataset labels;
- without an entry there: the website name of the folder README title ("# Airbnb.com Scraper"), the first
  scrapfly.io/blog article linked in that README, and a label made from the file name ("hotel_reviews.json" ->
  "Hotel reviews");
- the folder README.md: written from the entry above plus the code: the `scrape_*` functions with their return type
  and docstring, the TypedDict result types with their fields, the `BASE_CONFIG` settings, the tests of test.py and
  example calls found in run.py (calls with literal arguments only, lists cut to two items);
- sample datasets: every results/*.json file; functions (llms.txt): the scrape_* functions of the scraper code with
  the first paragraph of their docstring;
- status: the nightly badge that .github/scripts/ci_status.py writes to the ci-status branch, linked to the
  scraper's page of the last run there (each test with its job log).

Standard library only.
"""
import argparse
import ast
import json
import re
import sys
import warnings
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
REPO = "scrapfly/scrapfly-scrapers"
EXTRA = ROOT / ".github" / "scrapers.json"
GUIDE_LINK = re.compile(r"https://scrapfly\.io/blog/[^\s)\"'>]+")
# translated READMEs: the same table, links one folder up, column headers in that language
TRANSLATIONS = {
    "zh-CN": ("网站", "指南", "示例数据", "状态"),
    "ja": ("サイト", "ガイド", "サンプルデータ", "ステータス"),
    "es": ("Dominio", "Guía", "Datos de ejemplo", "Estado"),
    "ru": ("Сайт", "Руководство", "Примеры данных", "Статус"),
}
HEADERS = ("Domain", "Guide", "Sample Datasets", "Status")
TABLE_NOTE = (
    "<!-- This table is generated by .github/scripts/docs.py from the scraper folders and .github/scrapers.json: "
    "change those and run the script instead of editing it. -->"
)

README_NOTE = (
    "<!-- This README is generated by .github/scripts/docs.py from the scraper code, its results/ folder and "
    ".github/scrapers.json: change those and run the script instead of editing it. -->"
)

LLMS_HEADER = """\
# Scrapfly Scrapers

> Example web scrapers for {count} popular websites (Amazon, eBay, Zillow, Booking.com and more), written in \
Python with the Scrapfly SDK, one folder per website. Each scraper is a short module of async functions that \
return JSON-ready data, with an example script (`run.py`), live tests (`test.py`) and sample output (`results/`).

These scrapers are educational examples that accompany the Scrapfly scrape guides: copy one and adapt it, they are not \
an installable library. Every request goes through the Scrapfly API, so a Scrapfly API key is needed \
(https://scrapfly.io/register, then https://scrapfly.io/dashboard).

## When an agent should use this repository

- The user wants data from one of the websites listed under "Scrapers": start from that folder instead of writing a \
scraper from scratch.
- The user wants to scrape a similar website: copy the scraper of the closest website (same kind of pages: search \
results, product, property, profile, reviews) and adapt its URLs, parsing and tests.
- The user wants to learn how a website is scraped: most folders link a step-by-step guide.

## Use a scraper

```bash
git clone https://github.com/scrapfly/scrapfly-scrapers.git
cd scrapfly-scrapers/amazon-scraper        # any <name>-scraper folder
export SCRAPFLY_KEY="your API key"         # from https://scrapfly.io/dashboard
poetry install --no-root                   # Python 3.10+
poetry run python run.py                   # example scrape, writes results/*.json
poetry run pytest test.py -k test_search   # live tests, one test at a time with -k
```

From Python, inside the scraper folder:

```python
import asyncio
import amazon  # the module has the name of the folder without "-scraper"

results = asyncio.run(amazon.scrape_search("https://www.amazon.com/s?k=kindle", max_pages=1))
```

- The module reads `SCRAPFLY_KEY` from the environment when it is imported.
- In most modules `BASE_CONFIG` holds the Scrapfly settings sent with every request (anti-bot bypass, country, \
...); change an entry there (for example `BASE_CONFIG["cache"] = True`) rather than every request.
- The functions return plain dicts and lists; `results/` holds the output `run.py` produced with them.
- `run.py` and the tests scrape the live websites, so they use Scrapfly credits and fail when a website changes.

## Scrapers

Each entry: the folder, its scrape guide, its sample output and its public functions (`async`, run them with \
`asyncio.run` or `await`).

"""

